--- body ---
## Context

R22 promises one obvious way to stop all background work, holding across
restarts (`SPEC.md` R22). The acceptance layer registers two checks:
`R22-pause-stops-background-work` and
`R22-pause-holds-across-restart`. Their defensive machinery is valuable: they
do not trust the pause reply, require a never-paused positive control, separate
the graded and control state, distinguish immediate from restart persistence,
and report absent counters or perturbations as non-verdicts.

The checks nevertheless do not faithfully decide the full requirement:

1. Both wait for stationary counters before calling `pause`, then create work.
   They test future scheduling, not whether active workers drain or stop.
2. The restart arm deliberately opens its graded window only after the restarted
   target settles. Work that resumes automatically during startup is excluded,
   although R22's own rationale names that as the falsifier.
3. The zoteus-only fallback implements `RESUME_EMBEDDING` by explicitly calling
   `action:"build"` after pause. `SPEC.md §5.2.7` says pause does not gate
   explicit verbs (`build` while paused asks). The harness observes neither an
   ask nor consent and currently treats any resulting work as red. The two FAILs
   in `bench/results/0643-resume-embedding-perturbation/` therefore do not settle
   R22 as written; that artifact remains useful evidence that the counters and
   perturbation path execute.
4. One selected perturbation stands in for "all background work". A target can
   stop edits while continuing build, extraction, embedding, retries,
   reconciliation or upgrades and still pass.
5. Only positive `*.done` deltas are graded. Fetching, scanning, hashing,
   extraction, embedding before commit, retries, rollback loops, CPU, I/O and
   network activity can continue invisibly.
6. The restart arm covers a normal target-process cycle only: not crash,
   `kill -9`, host/plugin restart, reboot-equivalent cold start, upgrade, or the
   sideline/fresh-file path the design says carries the pause row.
7. The normalized adapter supplies one `pause` method, so uniqueness and
   obviousness of the real user control are never observed.
8. `resume` is never tested: clearing pause, idempotence, queued-work release,
   and the prohibition on forcing rebuild/refresh/repair/sync are uncovered.
9. Required exclusions are uncovered: pause must leave queries, probes and
   deletion propagation live. Since the verdict rejects every positive `done`
   delta regardless of trigger, coincident required deletion work can also
   manufacture a false red.
10. Control independence checks paths but not identical target identity,
    revision, configuration, fixture contents, enabled stages or checkpoint.

This is an acceptance-instrument finding, not a finding about whether current
zoteus keeps R22. Until the instrument is repaired and re-run, ticket 0613 must
not use the 0643 FAILs as its final R22 verdict.

## Master-ticket role

This ticket owns the R22 coverage inventory, decomposition, dependency
accounting, reconciliation of the 0643 evidence, and final integrated verdict.
It is not the implementation ticket for every missing arm. Create bounded child
tickets on demand, before implementation, and list them below.

## Work packages

### 1. Stop work already in flight

- Start each independently scheduled work class and prove it active before
  invoking pause: crawl/build, extraction, chunking, embedding, retry,
  reconciliation and key/prefix upgrade where the target offers them.
- Observe dispatch stopping, the specified drain/cancel boundary, zero workers,
  and the absence of post-boundary resource activity and commits.
- Use independent observables for activity that does not reach a `done`
  counter. Missing observability is not-run, never PASS.
- Give each independently falsifiable work class its own result; one supported
  candidate cannot stand for "all".

### 2. Prove durable pause across every relevant restart

- Grade work from the beginning of startup. An automatic resume before the
  first stationary status is a failure, not discarded warm-up.
- Exercise normal restart, abrupt death, a fresh process identity, host/plugin
  restart where available, upgrade/reopen, and the sideline/fresh-file path.
- Plant positive controls proving each lifecycle transition occurred and would
  resume work without the durable marker.
- Separate unavailable lifecycle substrates from green verdicts.

### 3. Test the control contract and its boundaries

- Establish how the real user discovers and invokes the single control; detect
  multiple independent switches or an adapter-normalized control users cannot
  find.
- Test `resume`: it clears pause, is idempotent, releases queued work, preserves
  committed progress, and never forces rebuild, refresh, repair or sync.
- While paused, prove queries and probes still answer and deletion propagation
  still runs. Attribute counters by trigger so required removal cannot be
  mistaken for forbidden derivation.
- Test explicit work verbs according to the specification's "asks" rule. The
  harness must observe the confirmation/consent boundary rather than equating
  an explicit request with unattended background scheduling.
- Require the positive control and graded target to match in target identity,
  revision, configuration, source fixture, enabled stages and initial state in
  addition to having disjoint paths.

### 4. Integrate and correct the evidence record

- Retain the existing reply-independent, positive-control, queued-work and
  not-run protections and their focused fail-controls.
- Replace the regression that blesses startup work with a control that drives
  automatic startup resume red.
- Reclassify the 0643 result honestly: preserve its raw evidence, but do not
  cite its explicit-build FAIL as an R22 verdict until a faithful arm reaches
  the same conclusion.
- Run the complete suite against zoteus at the reviewed baseline or a clearly
  labeled patch, commit raw evidence under `bench/results/`, and record PASS,
  FAIL or honest not-run in ticket 0613 and the public standing owner.

## Decomposition rule

Create children only as work becomes actionable. Each child owns one reviewable
instrument family, names 0665 as its master, carries its real `Blocked-by`
edges, and closes with red-first controls plus evidence. Machine-free fixture
work must not wait merely because a real target lacks a surface; machine- or
target-dependent arms carry that dependency themselves. Do not file a batch of
placeholder children and do not implement the work packages in this master.

## Children

| child | scope | state |
|---|---|---|
| — | none filed yet | spin off on demand |

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Every work package is allocated to bounded children or explicitly ruled
      out at the owning authority, and every child is closed.
- [ ] Pause is invoked during proven active work and all offered background-work
      classes receive independent verdicts.
- [ ] Startup work is inside the restart window, and every offered restart,
      crash, upgrade and sideline path is exercised with a positive control.
- [ ] Uniqueness/obviousness, resume semantics, required-live operations and
      explicit-verb consent have behavioral checks and red controls.
- [ ] Non-commit activity cannot hide behind flat `*.done` counters.
- [ ] The 0643 artifact is no longer represented as a faithful R22 verdict
      unless a corrected check supports it.
- [ ] A complete real-target artifact is committed and ticket 0613 records the
      integrated result without collapsing unsupported arms into green.
- [ ] `make check` and `./tickets/erg check tickets/` pass at master close.

## Depends on

None. This master blocks tracker 0613; its children carry only their own real
prerequisites.

## Invariants

- This ticket coordinates; children implement.
- One independently reviewable instrument family per child.
- `SPEC.md` owns every design number and `DECISIONS.md` precedes any changed
  interpretation.
- Fixture success proves the detector can fire, never that a real target keeps
  R22.
